  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 PM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nA quiet start to the week before an active pattern settles in \nthrough the end of the work week. Temperatures have warmed up quick \nafter morning fog burned off. Highs will top out a few degrees \neither side of normal for mid-August. A few very isolated showers \ncannot be ruled out this afternoon and evening, but most locations \nshould stay dry today. Looking aloft, northwest flow will dominate \nthe upper air pattern over the next 7 days. Our current airmass will \nremain locked into the region with surface dew points in the upper \n60s to mid 70s and highs near-normal. This will provide enough \ninstability for near daily thunderstorm and shower chances Tuesday \nthrough the end of the work week.  \n\nThere is some uncertainty with the potential for a decaying MCS \nTuesday morning to move into our Virginia and northeastern TN \ncounties. There is higher confidence for an additional MCS to impact \nthe northern forecast area Tuesday afternoon and evening. A potent \nshortwave will cross through the S Great Lakes and Ohio Valley \nregion mid day Tuesday. This will provide additional forcing and \nsupport for a line of thunderstorms (MCS) to develop upstream over \nthe Ohio Valley that will track southeast and eventually southward \ninto our northern forecast area Tuesday afternoon/evening. \nUnfortunately, this is not a confidence forecast for \nplacement/location given the potential for the morning convection \nthat could impact where boundaries and gradients set up, which will \nimpact where afternoon TS develops/tracks. There is moderate \nconfidence on timing for this primary round. \n\nForecast soundings from hi-res guidance support non-zero bulk \nshear that would support potential for isolated severe weather. \nHRRR soundings highlight a favorable environment for damaging \nwinds with storms on Tuesday. This would make sense given the \npotential MCS or linear storm mode. There is also a non-zero \nchance for a tornado or two in our Virginia counties. This lines \nup with the latest SPC outlook for Day two. A Slight risk (level 2\nof 5) in our Virginia area with a Marginal (1 of 5) down into our\nTN area. Flash flooding and flooding cannot be ruled out given \nthe potential for multiple rounds of thunderstorms and heavy rain \ntracking over the northern forecast area in Virginia and NE \nTennessee. The signal from the 12Z guidance for the heaviest \nrainfall remains north of our forecast area.\n\nWednesday through Friday look similar with respect to the forecast. \nNorthwest flow persists with the potential of afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms. Severe threat will be low, but non-zero with wind\nas the primary hazard. Heavy rain will also remain a concern this\nweek. Any location that receives multiple rounds of rain will have\na greater risk of flooding. This pattern supports MCS formation, \nbut the further out in time, the greater the uncertainty as far as\ntiming, extent, and impacts. The upper level pattern begins to \nchange for the weekend as heights increase as an upper level \nridge begins to build into the region. This will support \ntemperatures to trend a few degrees warmer than normal Saturday \nand Sunday.",
